#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130030} :activity/id #uuid "f74c4548-dfc9-5c4c-bb83-8b7024f32b7f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050546 :topic/id #uuid "5269dbdc-fe3c-5ce7-b18c-f9aa0c7f6711" :topic/math-academy-id 2000} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130030 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130375 :tutorial/content "Double integrals have properties that are similar to the properties of definite integrals of single-variable functions:\n\n- **Linearity of Double Integrals.** Suppose that  $f(x,y)$ and $g(x,y)$ are continuous functions on a domain $D$ and that $\\alpha, \\beta$ are constants. Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} \\left[\\alpha f(x,y) + \\beta g(x,y)\\right] \\ \\textrm d A = \\alpha\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y)   \\ \\textrm d A + \\beta\\iint\\limits_{D} g(x,y)   \\ \\textrm d A.\n$$\n\n- **The Double Integral of Unity.** The integral of $f(x,y) = 1$ over a domain $D$ is simply the area of $D\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{D}  \\ \\textrm d A = \\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{D} 1 \\cdot \\textrm d A = \\textrm{Area}\\ \\!(D).\n$$\n\nOther properties are more specific to double integrals. \n\n- **The Multiplicative Property of Double Integrals.** If $f(x)$ is continuous on $[a,b]$ and $g(y)$ is continuous on $[c,d]$ then \n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{R}  f(x)g(y) \\ \\textrm d A = \\left(\\int_a^b f(x)  \\ \\textrm d x\\right)\\left( \\int_c^d g(y) \\ \\textrm d y\\right),\n$$\n\nwhere $R= [a,b] \\times [c,d].$" :tutorial/id #uuid "6158466e-1222-52a1-88ad-028b5c5832c5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3860 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "15730ddd-c579-5f89-bff8-85f42db15b97" :step/math-academy-id 22622 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130031}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130031 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129170 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128989 :question/id #uuid "53185b25-c690-500a-9a82-c18b37668e27" :question/math-academy-id "e-6106"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec44686a-601b-52b3-81df-e57e6708fb93" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050207}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129845} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129846} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265786} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265787} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348401} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348403} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380852} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380859} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380866} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382393}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Double Integral Using the Multiplicative Property"} :step/id #uuid "2526c89c-75e6-5851-84b4-83ae4eaabc04" :step/math-academy-id 22623 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130032}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130032 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130376 :tutorial/content "We can also apply the multiplicative property when the integrand contains only one of the variables. \n\nFor example, suppose that $R= [a,b] \\times [c,d].$ Then, we have the general result\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}f(x)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{R}(f(x))(1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =({\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x)({\\int }_{c}^{d}1\\,\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =({\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x)(d-c) \\\\  & =(d-c){\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nBy the same reasoning, we also have \n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{R}  g(y) \\, \\textrm{d}A = (b-a) \\int_c^d g(y) \\, \\textrm{d}y.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "3acdc936-b0cf-5aca-a57a-00bdc558bb88" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3861 :tutorial/title "Applying the Multiplicative Property when the Integrand Contains Only One Variable"} :step/id #uuid "12e3fe87-21fe-5661-a2ed-48880eba8e48" :step/math-academy-id 22624 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130033}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130033 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129171 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128990 :question/id #uuid "a5567cb9-1c16-56a6-b0e8-759edf693a41" :question/math-academy-id "e-6107"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "307bccb6-036f-51bb-99ce-327aad8dfdbe" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129847} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129848} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348423} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348424} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348427} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351275} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351276} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362483} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380873} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380880} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382407} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382448}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Double Integral When the Integrand Contains Only One Variable"} :step/id #uuid "e720a516-990b-5dfb-b236-aca8fdfa01d1" :step/math-academy-id 22625 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130068}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130034 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129172 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128991 :question/id #uuid "0cb8fa8a-5be1-5f53-bfde-5acf325f6332" :question/math-academy-id "e-6108"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8059a197-e9fb-5306-8eed-62c0cea88f33" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049534} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050432}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129849} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129872} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265804} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265805} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265807} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265808} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351279} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380908} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382193} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382462}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Double Integral Using the Additivity Over a Rectangular Domain"} :step/id #uuid "f422ea91-80bb-5373-9f64-444e0646074d" :step/math-academy-id 22627} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130068 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130397 :tutorial/content "Remember that the definite integral of a single-variable function can be split up over adjacent subintervals. That is to say,\n\n$$\n\\int_a^c f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = \\int_a^b f(x) \\, \\textrm dx + \\int_b^c f(x) \\, \\textrm dx.\n$$\n\nIn the same way, the definite integral of a two-variable function can be split up over adjacent sub-domains.\n\nSuppose that a domain $D$ is the union of two adjacent sub-domains $D_1$ and $D_2$ (meaning that $D_1$ and $D_2$ are non-overlapping regions that could share a common border). Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D}  f(x,y)\\ \\textrm{d}A = \\iint\\limits_{D_1}  f(x,y)\\ \\textrm{d}A + \\iint\\limits_{D_2}  f(x,y)\\ \\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nThis property can be generalized if $D$ is split into a finite number $n$ of sub-domains (where $n>2$):\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D}  f(x,y)\\ \\textrm{d}A = \\iint\\limits_{D_1}  f(x,y)\\ \\textrm{d}A + \\iint\\limits_{D_2}  f(x,y)\\ \\textrm{d}A + \\cdots + \\iint\\limits_{D_n}  f(x,y)\\ \\textrm{d}A\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "bc4e2a2c-afd3-57e1-ac95-737c568153b8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4258 :tutorial/title "Additivity of Double Integrals"} :step/id #uuid "5249980b-32b5-5965-880e-a7948ef2b5f3" :step/math-academy-id 25886 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130069}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130069 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129188 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129007 :question/id #uuid "1c227b12-d40b-53a8-b568-b7bd380acbee" :question/math-academy-id "e-7023"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8505beff-75b8-57bf-9312-e0fcf32f44fd" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129873} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129874} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265799} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348431} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348432} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380887} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380894} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380901} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382455} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394030} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419654}] :knowledge-point/title "Using Additivity of Double Integrals"} :step/id #uuid "bca38320-513b-5017-88bb-f5d1a8c6013e" :step/math-academy-id 25887 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130070}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130070 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130398 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we're integrating a single-variable function over a region $R.$ The definite integral of $f(x)$ over $R$ is not affected by whether the region $R$ is open or closed.\n\nFor example, consider the function $f(x) = 1,$ and define the regions $R = [0,1]$ and $R' = (0,1).$ Integrating $f(x)$ over $R$ and $R'$ gives the same result:\n\n$$\n\\int_R f(x) \\,\\textrm d x = \\int_{R'} f(x) \\,\\textrm d x = \\int_{0}^1 \\,\\textrm d x = 1\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this is true because the rectangles formed by $f(x),$ namely $[0, 1]\\times [0,1]$ and $(0, 1)\\times (0,1),$ have the same area. \n\nWe have a similar property for double integrals too. We can either include or exclude the boundaries of the integration regions without affecting the result. \n\nFor example, the double integral of $f(x,y)=x^2+y^2$ over both $R=[0,1] \\times [0,1]$ and over $R'=(0,1) \\times (0,1)$ will give the same result:\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{\\large R} (x^2+y^2) \\ \\text{d}A = \\iint\\limits_{\\large R'} (x^2+y^2) \\ \\text{d}A= \\int_{0}^{1} \\int_{0}^{1} (x^2+y^2) \\ \\text{d}A\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this is true because the *volumes* of the shapes formed by $f(x,y)$ over the regions $R$ and $R'$ are the same.\n\nMoreover, we'd get the same result if we were to integrate $f(x,y)$ over any of the following regions:\n\n$$\n(0,1] \\times [0,1], \\quad [0,1) \\times [0,1], \\quad (0,1) \\times [0,1], \\quad \\ldots\n$$\n\nThe same idea applies to double-integrals over non-rectangular boundaries, too." :tutorial/id #uuid "7f51d452-a229-5d76-885e-0fe84f787707" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4311 :tutorial/title "Open and Closed Regions of Integration"} :step/id #uuid "45710b0f-af68-5a20-b9ce-c5ecc560f4e5" :step/math-academy-id 26202 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130034}}] :activity/title "Properties of Double Integrals" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186129345}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419655} :activity/id #uuid "abdc4a65-40f3-528e-9141-f38a1d8b8f37" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050546 :topic/id #uuid "5269dbdc-fe3c-5ce7-b18c-f9aa0c7f6711" :topic/math-academy-id 2000} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419655 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129848 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1dc8e0ee-42d1-5e33-ab3e-d149ce209193" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128574} {:answer/id #uuid "216a895e-32d8-5d87-938d-db65b2d1bddc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "19e-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419645} {:answer/id #uuid "4a5a35ab-9584-5250-9b05-bab514871947" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "19-20e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419646} {:answer/id #uuid "c482d14a-968e-5fba-92dc-75544ab448dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20-19e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419647} {:answer/id #uuid "c6534224-e681-59ea-874f-2267428a83f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16+20e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419648}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419647} :answer-field/id #uuid "5432046c-908f-53bb-aa5e-d0f6af32c211" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128569}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a9bb5733-5b86-5301-9267-6ae190810785" :question/math-academy-id "q-71994" :question/problem "Evaluate the double integral ${∬}_{R}\\frac{1-3x{y}^{2}}{x}\\,\\text{d}A$,  where $R=[1,e]\\times [2,3].$" :question/worked-solution "Using the linearity property of double integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}\\frac{1-3x{y}^{2}}{x}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{R}(\\frac{1}{x}-3{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{R}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}A-{∬}_{R}3{y}^{2}\\text{ }\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function under the first integral is independent of $y.$ So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}\\frac{1}{x}\\text{ }\\text{d}A & =(3-2){\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|]_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function under the second integral is independent of $x.$ So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}3{y}^{2}\\text{ }\\text{d}A & =(e-1){\\int }_{2}^{3}3{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =(e-1)[{y}^{3}]_{2}^{3} \\\\  & =(e-1)({3}^{3}-{2}^{3}) \\\\  & =19(e-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, combining our results, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}\\frac{1-3x{y}^{2}}{x}\\,\\text{d}A=1-19(e-1)=20-19e.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8b0d3e0f-96a8-599d-92f9-04874e8f5b73" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419656}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419656 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129873 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60fb41e7-e3b3-596b-9de2-56afe6dd5f07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{∬}_{S}x\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{U}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346193} {:answer/id #uuid "6c9ef82d-4456-5d25-ad26-90ed8d7f4c1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{∬}_{S}x\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{T}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346295} {:answer/id #uuid "705f8690-2e07-5932-b07c-8089792360e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{∬}_{T}x\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{U}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346333} {:answer/id #uuid "9666eb71-130a-59fc-8098-2ba78743d703" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{∬}_{S}x\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{T}y\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{U}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346666} {:answer/id #uuid "c006f721-3d51-5d16-a6a8-1b8222268571" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{∬}_{S}x\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{T}(x+{x}^{2}y)\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{U}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347011}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186346193} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ef450e5-1471-5ba8-8652-38a2e4337160" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347853}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3e01cd29-3b44-5c08-bc75-551f046b44dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-80452" :question/problem "![](images/ed/ed0be2aca8d43d733e91f3a8feaaca0649957dcbd516a8218b1e7dea136170d8.png)\n\nConsider the region $R$ shown above. Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}x, & (x,y)\\in S, \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in T, \\\\ {x}^{2}y, & (x,y)\\in U.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n$$\n{∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the domain $R$ can be represented as a union of three sub-domains, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & =S\\cup T\\cup U.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{S}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{T}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{U}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}x\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{T}0\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{U}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}x\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{U}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b57250fe-0a51-501d-8a2f-9f787d4986a6" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419657}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419657 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419654 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "168581f2-f17c-5d44-a38c-f447560aea9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419644} {:answer/id #uuid "c6d9732d-dbc5-596f-ab65-73c4ce86f71e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419649} {:answer/id #uuid "d0b906bf-abf8-5f7b-aa2d-46eb0fb79934" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419650} {:answer/id #uuid "da997437-2a62-59ee-9431-4e9d511ef420" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419651} {:answer/id #uuid "f67323c5-5cdb-58d8-a3cb-89a513553c59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419652}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419644} :answer-field/id #uuid "35e876f8-837f-5088-b5c0-91f0a9d62487" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419653}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a49df285-b000-551a-958c-a18cbfff682c" :question/math-academy-id "q-80659" :question/problem "Given that $C$ and $D$ are finite, non-overlapping regions and that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{C}f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A=-3,\\,{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A=5,\n$$\n\nfind the value of \n\n$$\n{∬}_{C\\cup D}-2f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the linearity and additivity of double integrals, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{C\\cup D}-2f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A & =-2\\cdot \\,\\,\\,{∬}_{C\\cup D}f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-2\\cdot ({∬}_{C}f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A) \\\\  & =-2\\cdot (-3+5) \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "f337f9e2-418d-5c24-9790-11f1621a546e"}] :activity/title "Properties of Double Integrals" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419643}]}
